{"id":"ara-t0-0001","lang":"ara","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.hello","domain":1,"text_native":"سَلاَم","text_romanized":"salām","text_english":"Hello.","source_caption":"Hello","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The default greeting anywhere. Works with every age and every status.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Arabic phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Arabic_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"ara-t0-0002","lang":"ara","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.morning","domain":1,"text_native":"صباح الخير","text_romanized":"sabaH al-khair","text_english":"Good morning.","source_caption":"Good morning.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Used until roughly midday. Getting the time of day wrong is noticed; not saying it is only noticed by someone expecting it.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Arabic phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Arabic_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"ara-t0-0003","lang":"ara","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.afternoon","domain":1,"text_native":"بعد ظهر","text_romanized":"ba'ada Zuhur","text_english":"Good afternoon.","source_caption":"afternoon","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The middle of the day. In several languages this is the same word as the evening greeting, and using it late is not an error.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Arabic phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Arabic_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"ara-t0-0004","lang":"ara","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.evening","domain":1,"text_native":"مساء الخير","text_romanized":"masaa' al-khair","text_english":"Good evening.","source_caption":"Good evening.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"From late afternoon. Often interchangeable with the afternoon greeting.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Arabic phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Arabic_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"ara-t0-0005","lang":"ara","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.i-am-well","domain":1,"text_native":"بخير ,شكرا","text_romanized":"bi-khair, shukran","text_english":"I am well, thank you.","source_caption":"Fine, thank you.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The expected answer to the greeting above. Answering it wrong reads as evasive in cultures where it is a formula.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Arabic phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Arabic_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"ara-t0-0006","lang":"ara","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.nice-to-meet-you","domain":1,"text_native":"تشرفنا","text_romanized":"tasharafna lit. \"You honor us\"","text_english":"Nice to meet you.","source_caption":"Nice to meet you.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Said on a first introduction. In several languages there is a separate phrase for meeting someone formally that a stranger will use instead.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Arabic phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Arabic_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"ara-t0-0007","lang":"ara","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.where-from","domain":1,"text_native":"من اين انت","text_romanized":"min ayn anta","text_english":"I am from","source_caption":"Where are you from?","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The question after your name, and the answer to it. Where you are from changes how you are treated, so it gets asked early.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Arabic phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Arabic_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"ara-t0-0008","lang":"ara","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.please","domain":1,"text_native":"من فضلك","text_romanized":"min faDlak","text_english":"Please.","source_caption":"Please.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Not a filler in English, and a grammatical particle in some of these languages — one word that changes a sentence from an order to a request.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Arabic phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Arabic_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"ara-t0-0009","lang":"ara","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.thank-you","domain":1,"text_native":"شُكْرًا جَزِيلًا (","text_romanized":"''shukran jazīlan''","text_english":"Thank you.","source_caption":"Thank you very much!","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The single most useful word in any of these languages. Saying it costs nothing and changes how everything after it is received.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Arabic phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Arabic_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"ara-t0-0010","lang":"ara","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.youre-welcome","domain":1,"text_native":"عَفْوًا","text_romanized":"'afwaan","text_english":"You are welcome.","source_caption":"You're welcome.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"You will be handed it constantly, and there is no way to avoid replying. In a few languages the reply is the same word as 'yes'.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Arabic phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Arabic_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"ara-t0-0011","lang":"ara","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.excuse-me","domain":1,"text_native":"إسمحلي","text_romanized":"ismaHli","text_english":"Excuse me.","source_caption":"Excuse me.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Two different things in one phrase: getting someone's attention, and getting past them. Guessing wrong is jarring — one version walks through a conversation.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Arabic phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Arabic_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"ara-t0-0012","lang":"ara","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.sorry","domain":1,"text_native":"انا اسفه / انا اسف","text_romanized":"ana aasif (male) / ana aasifah","text_english":"I am sorry.","source_caption":"I'm sorry.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Apologising for a small fault — bumping someone, being late, not understanding. The form you use signals how much of a fault you think it was.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Arabic phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Arabic_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"ara-t0-0013","lang":"ara","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.one-moment","domain":1,"text_native":"لحظة","text_romanized":"/laH-dha","text_english":"One moment, please.","source_caption":"Just a moment.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Buys you time to think, find a word, or finish what you were doing — without the silence that reads as rude or as not understanding.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Arabic phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Arabic_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"ara-t0-0014","lang":"ara","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.goodbye","domain":1,"text_native":"مع السلامة","text_romanized":"ma`a as-salaamah","text_english":"Goodbye.","source_caption":"Goodbye","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"How you leave. Leaving without one is noticeably rude in most of these languages.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Arabic phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Arabic_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"ara-t0-0015","lang":"ara","tier":0,"slot":"repair.dont-understand","domain":12,"text_native":"لا افهم","text_romanized":"laa afham","text_english":"I do not understand.","source_caption":"I don't understand.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The one phrase that keeps a conversation alive. Without it people stop trying, and with it they often rephrase in simpler words.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Arabic phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Arabic_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"ara-t0-0016","lang":"ara","tier":0,"slot":"bridge.speak-english","domain":12,"text_native":"هل تتكلم الانجليزية","text_romanized":"hal tatakallam al-ingliziyyah?","text_english":"Do you speak English?","source_caption":"Do you speak English?","register":"neutral","direction":"understand","why":"Asked of you constantly, and the question that decides whether anything after it can happen. There is a polite version and a blunt version and the gap between them is wide.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Arabic phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Arabic_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
